@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# SPEC: CSV import of contacts
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. They want to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one, and to see what happened: what was created, what
+was updated, and which rows need fixing. The file has the same columns as our CSV export
+(`name,email,phone,tags`). Files from our address-book export, which has `first_name` and
+`last_name` instead of `name`, must also work.
+
+An import must never lose data that is already in the book. Rows that match existing
+contacts are merged into them; the existing contacts are not replaced or wiped.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Line number**: the row number a spreadsheet shows for that line. The header is row 1.
+  Every line of the file counts, blank lines included.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a row where every cell is empty or contains only whitespace.
+- **Contact row**: any data row (after the header) that is not blank.
+- **Blank cell**: a cell that is empty or contains only whitespace. A missing optional column
+  counts as a blank cell on every row.
+- Validation and normalisation of names, emails, phones and tags are the existing ones in
+  `validation.py` / `Contact.create`. The import adds no rules of its own.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Input and decoding
+
+1. `data` may be `bytes` or `str`. Both give the same result for the same content.
+2. Bytes are decoded as UTF-8, with or without a leading BOM.
+3. Text input that starts with the BOM character (U+FEFF) is accepted. The BOM is not part
+   of the first header name.
+4. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`. Its message tells the user to
+   re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+5. The delimiter is a comma. Quoting follows standard CSV, as produced by `export_csv`.
+   Other delimiters do not have to be supported.
+
+### Header
+
+6. The first line is a header row. Column order is free.
+7. Header names are matched case-insensitively, ignoring surrounding whitespace
+   (e.g. ` Email ` matches `email`).
+8. `email` is a required column. If it is missing, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+9. A name column is required: either `name`, or both `first_name` and `last_name`. If
+   neither is present, the import raises `ImportFileError`.
+10. `phone` and `tags` are optional columns. If they are missing, the file is still imported.
+11. Unknown extra columns are ignored. They never cause an error or reject a row. They may
+    produce a warning.
+12. An empty file (no content, or nothing but whitespace or blank lines) raises `ImportFileError`.
+13. A file with only a header row returns a report whose four lists are all empty.
+
+### Whole-file limits
+
+14. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError`. The message tells the
+    user to split the file. Invalid rows count toward the limit; blank rows do not. Exactly
+    1000 contact rows are accepted.
+15. Whenever `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is left exactly as it was: no
+    contact is added or changed.
+16. `ImportFileError` is a subclass of `ContactError`.
+
+### Rows
+
+17. Blank rows are skipped silently. They produce no error or warning, and they still count
+    toward line numbers.
+18. Each `RowIssue.row` is the line number of the row it is about.
+19. For an issue about one field, `RowIssue.column` is the lower-case column name: `name`,
+    `email`, `phone` or `tags`. For problems with the whole row, `column` may be anything,
+    including `None`.
+20. Each row is either applied in full or not applied at all. A row with an error does not
+    change the repository at all, not even partly for an existing contact. The single
+    exception is criterion 30, an invalid phone, which is a warning, not an error.
+21. Good rows are imported even when other rows in the same file have errors (partial import).
+22. A row whose email is missing or invalid is an error with `column="email"`.
+23. A row whose name is missing or invalid is an error with `column="name"`.
+24. With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is the two cells joined by a single space. If one
+    of the two cells is blank, the name is the other cell alone, with no leading or trailing
+    space. If both are blank, the row is an error with `column="name"`.
+25. Tags are split on `;`. Empty pieces (e.g. `vip;;lead`, a trailing `;`) and repeated tags
+    are ignored. If any tag in the cell is invalid, the row is an error with `column="tags"`.
+26. A row may produce more than one error, e.g. one per bad field.
+
+### New vs existing contacts
+
+27. Emails are matched after normalisation, so `Ann@Example.COM` in the file matches
+    `ann@example.com` in the book.
+28. A valid row whose email is not in the repository creates a contact. Its normalised email
+    is listed in `created`.
+29. A valid row whose email is already in the repository updates that contact. It is never
+    an error, and it never creates a second contact. The result is a merge:
+    - The name is replaced by the file's name.
+    - The phone is replaced only when the phone cell is non-blank and valid. A blank phone
+      cell, or a missing `phone` column, keeps the existing phone.
+    - Tags from the file are added to the existing tags. An import never removes tags.
+    - The normalised email is listed in `updated`. (Whether an update that changes nothing
+      is listed in `updated` is left open.)
+30. An invalid phone (non-blank but rejected by validation) does not cost us the contact.
+    The rest of the row is applied as usual. The phone is left as it was: an existing
+    contact keeps its current phone, and a new contact gets no phone. A warning with `column="phone"` is added for that row. It is not an error.
+31. If the same normalised email appears in more than one contact row, the first such row is
+    processed normally. Each later row with that email is an error on that later row, and
+    it is not applied. (When the first row with an email has an error, whether it still
+    counts as the first row is left open.)
+32. `created` and `updated` contain only normalised emails. No email appears more than once
+    across the two lists.
+33. Warnings never stop a row from being applied. A row that is not applied is not listed in
+    `created` or `updated`.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Excel/xlsx or any format other than CSV.
+- Encodings other than UTF-8; there is no fallback encoding.
+- Delimiters other than a comma.
+- Deleting or otherwise changing contacts that are not in the file.
+- Removing tags or clearing phones through an import.
+- A dry-run or preview mode.
+- Wiring into the HTTP API, CLI or any UI.
+
+## Left open (implementer's choice; tests must not pin these)
+
+- Using `name` vs `first_name`/`last_name` when the header has both, and whether only one of
+  `first_name`/`last_name` without `name` is accepted.
+- Duplicated header names.
+- Duplicate emails in the file where the first row with that email has an error: whether that
+  row still counts as "first", making the later rows errors, or the first valid later row
+  takes its place.
+- Rows with fewer or more cells than the header.
+- Broken CSV quoting: whether it is a row error or an `ImportFileError`.
+- Wording of messages, beyond criteria 4 and 14.
+- Warnings other than the invalid-phone warning in criterion 30.
+- Whether a no-op update is listed in `updated`.
+- The `column` value for whole-row issues.
+- The order of entries in the report lists.
